tags: germany

= Artists and Intellectuals

Revolution is fashionable in the cafés of Berlin. George Grosz draws generals and profiteers with pigs' faces; John Heartfield and his brother Wieland Herzfelde print them at the Malik-Verlag. [? if time >= 2 : Käthe Kollwitz has drawn Liebknecht on his bier. ?] [? if year >= 1920 : Erwin Piscator has opened a Proletarian Theatre in the workers' halls. ?] Professors and students, some of them veterans, come to our meetings and write to our papers.

Some comrades think the artists will be useful. Others think they are tourists.

- @welcome: Open the party to them.
- @workers_culture: Build a culture of the workers themselves.
- @posters: Put them to work for the press.
- @easy_discard

@welcome
subtitle: New members, new ideas and donations from the comfortable. Some workers are suspicious.
on-arrival: resources += 1; worker_support += 1; unity -= 2; luxemburgist_satisfaction += 4; lean_luxemburgist += 1

The party's meetings fill with people in good coats. Their donations pay for the printing; their arguments go on until midnight.

@workers_culture
subtitle: Choirs, theatre and sport clubs, made by workers for workers. The Left approves.
on-arrival: worker_support += 2; militancy += 1; unity += 2; offensive_satisfaction += 3; lean_offensive += 1

Workers' choirs sing the Internationale in four voices, and amateur theatres play the Paris Commune in back rooms. It belongs to the workers, and they know it.

@posters
subtitle: Costs 1 fund. Brilliant posters and caricatures. Worker support rises.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no funds.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; worker_support += 4; militancy += 2

Heartfield's montages and Grosz's drawings appear on every hoarding in Berlin. The police tear them down in the morning; by evening there are new ones.
